    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>To choose the correct counting formula, ask three key questions: <i>Are all items being arranged, or only some of them?</i>, <i>Is any position fixed?</i>, and <i>Are two independent groups being arranged together?</i></p>

<ul>
  <li><b>All items arranged (\(n!\)):</b><br>
  In <b>Scenario 3</b>, all \({@n3@}\) students stand in a single row with no fixed positions, giving \({@n3@}! = {@val3@}\) arrangements &rarr; <b>Formula {@corr3@}</b>.</li>

  <li><b>One position fixed (\((n - 1)!\)):</b><br>
  In <b>Scenario 4</b>, the captain is fixed in the last batting position, so only the remaining \({@n4@} - 1 = {@rem4@}\) players need to be ordered, giving \(({@n4@} - 1)! = {@rem4@}! = {@val4@}\) arrangements &rarr; <b>Formula {@corr4@}</b>.</li>

  <li><b>Only some items arranged in order (\(\displaystyle {}_nP_r = \frac{n!}{(n - r)!}\)):</b>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Scenario 1:</b> Forming a \({@r1@}\)-digit code in order from \(10\) distinct digits (\(0\) to \(9\)) gives \(\displaystyle {}_{10}P_{{@r1@}} = \frac{10!}{(10 - {@r1@})!} = \frac{10!}{{@rem1@}!} = {@val1@}\) &rarr; <b>Formula {@corr1@}</b>.</li>
      <li><b>Scenario 2:</b> Awarding \({@r2@}\) distinct ranked prizes among \({@n2@}\) contestants gives \(\displaystyle {}_{{@n2@}}P_{{@r2@}} = \frac{{@n2@}!}{({@n2@} - {@r2@})!} = \frac{{@n2@}!}{{@rem2@}!} = {@val2@}\) &rarr; <b>Formula {@corr2@}</b>.</li>
      <li><b>Scenario 6:</b> Selecting an ordered playlist of \({@r6@}\) songs from \({@n6@}\) songs gives \(\displaystyle {}_{{@n6@}}P_{{@r6@}} = \frac{{@n6@}!}{({@n6@} - {@r6@})!} = \frac{{@n6@}!}{{@rem6@}!} = {@val6@}\) &rarr; <b>Formula {@corr6@}</b>.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>

  <li><b>Two independent groups arranged together (Fundamental Counting Principle):</b><br>
  In <b>Scenario 5</b>, selecting \({@L5@}\) different letters from \(26\) (\({}_{26}P_{{@L5@}}\)) followed by \({@D5@}\) different digits from \(10\) (\({}_{10}P_{{@D5@}}\)) gives \(\displaystyle {}_{26}P_{{@L5@}} \times {}_{10}P_{{@D5@}}\) &rarr; <b>Formula {@corr5@}</b>.</li>
</ul>

<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.1em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \text{1} \to \text{{@corr1@}}, \quad \text{2} \to \text{{@corr2@}}, \quad \text{3} \to \text{{@corr3@}}, \quad \text{4} \to \text{{@corr4@}}, \quad \text{5} \to \text{{@corr5@}}, \quad \text{6} \to \text{{@corr6@}} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
